# Copy to .env and adjust as needed.
#
# The calculation engines require no secrets. The Lighthouse cross-product
# integration below does — but every flag defaults to DISABLED, and none of the
# Lighthouse variables is wired into a running route today.
#
# The "public HTTP surface" section immediately below is the exception: those
# variables ARE live on every request. They are all optional, and every one of
# them defaults to the behaviour this service had before they existed.

# Port the HTTP server listens on. Must be an integer 1-65535.
# Defaults to 3001 if unset.
PORT=3001

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public HTTP surface — CORS, rate limiting, and the engine auth flag
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These govern the ~76 stateless calculation routes under /v1 (src/routes/**),
# which until now had no rate limit and no authentication at all. Everything
# here is optional. Unset means "behave exactly as this service did before",
# with one deliberate exception: the /v1 rate limit is ON by default, because a
# protective control that is off until configured is a control that is absent
# in every environment nobody remembered to configure.

# Comma-separated list of EXACT origins allowed to read responses from a
# browser. Example:
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.investscape.ca,http://localhost:5173
#
# UNSET (the default) = every origin is allowed, which is what `cors()` did
# before, and the server prints a loud startup warning saying so. That is not an
# oversight: an empty allow-list would not "fail closed", it would break the
# existing HTML prototype and every developer's localhost, in exchange for no
# confidentiality — the /v1 engines are unauthenticated and stateless today, so
# a cross-origin read returns arithmetic the caller could have done itself.
#
# SET = only those origins are allowed. Matching is exact string equality
# against scheme://host[:port]; there is no wildcard and no subdomain suffix
# matching (".example.com" also matches "evil-example.com" — a real, recurring
# bypass). A literal "*" is NOT accepted as an entry and is discarded with an
# error; if you want everything, unset this variable, which is loud, rather than
# hiding it inside a comma-separated string, which is not. Entries with a path
# or trailing slash are normalised down to the origin and the rewrite is logged.
# If the variable is set but no entry parses, the result is ZERO allowed
# origins, not a fallback to permissive.
#
# Note what this can and cannot do: CORS is enforced by the BROWSER. curl and
# every non-browser client ignore it. It limits what a malicious PAGE can read;
# it is not an access control on this server. Cookies/credentials are
# deliberately NOT enabled (`credentials: true` is unset) because this service
# authenticates with an Authorization: Bearer header, which needs no such
# permission — see src/http/cors.ts.
#
# FLIP THIS TO A REAL ALLOW-LIST BEFORE: enabling
# INVESTSCAPE_FF_REQUIRE_ENGINE_AUTH below, or introducing any cookie-based
# session. At that point permissive-on-unset stops being defensible.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Per-caller budget for /v1, using the same fixed-window limiter the Lighthouse
# routes use (src/lighthouse/http/rateLimit.ts). Defaults: 600 requests per
# 60000 ms. That is deliberately generous — one full multi-engine analysis fans
# out across many of the 52 route families at once, so 600/min is roughly 20
# complete recomputes a minute, far above any human driving a UI and far below a
# scraping loop. A limit set too low breaks the calculators and gets the whole
# control removed; a limit set somewhat too high still converts an unbounded
# flood into a bounded one.
#
# An invalid value (non-integer, or out of range) is IGNORED with a startup
# error and the default is used. It never resolves to "unlimited", and it never
# exits the process — a typo in a tuning knob must not take the engines down.
#
# Set ENGINE_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=0 to close /v1 entirely (every request gets a 429).
# That is the intended emergency brake if the engines must be shut off without
# a deploy.
#
# CAVEATS THAT MATTER IN PRODUCTION: the store is per-PROCESS and in-memory, so
# N instances behind a load balancer means N x this budget, and every deploy
# resets every counter. A fixed window also permits a burst of nearly 2x the
# limit across a window boundary — halve the window, not the limit, if that
# matters. This is not a substitute for an edge/WAF limit and is no defence
# against a distributed source.
# ENGINE_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=600
# ENGINE_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000

# How many reverse proxies YOU operate in front of this process, each appending
# one entry to X-Forwarded-For. DEFAULT 0, MEANING THE HEADER IS IGNORED
# ENTIRELY, because any client can send X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.<random> and
# rotate it every request — trusting it without a known hop count does not
# weaken the limiter, it deletes it.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING BEHIND A PROXY. With 0 hops behind a load balancer,
# every request keys to the balancer's address, so all traffic shares ONE budget
# and real users will 429 each other. Set this to the number of hops you
# actually control (usually 1). If you cannot state that number, leave it at 0.
#
# Applies to the /v1 engine limiter only. The Lighthouse per-route limiters
# still hardcode 0 hops; unifying them is a separate change.
# ENGINE_RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Require a verified session (Authorization: Bearer <jwt>) on the /v1
# calculation routes. Same "true"-only rule as the Lighthouse flags below.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: server-authoritative entitlement gating (Free/Pro/Enterprise)
# is impossible on an endpoint that does not know who is calling. Tier limits
# enforced in the browser are not limits. The engines must eventually require
# identity, so the mechanism is built, reviewed and testable NOW rather than
# improvised under commercial pressure later.
#
# WHY IT IS OFF: nothing consumes this API with a session yet. The HTML
# prototype sends no Authorization header and there is no token-issuing client.
# Enabling this today is a total outage of every existing caller in exchange for
# a gate nobody can pass. With it off, /v1 behaves byte-identically to before.
#
# Blocked on: (1) a front end that actually authenticates (Supabase session),
# (2) the entitlement/tier model this gate is the precondition for, and (3)
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS above being set to a real list.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED NOTE: if this is "true" while no session verifier is configured
# (no SUPABASE_JWT_ISSUER + JWKS/secret below), /v1 answers 503 for everyone. It
# does not fall back to allowing unauthenticated callers. Verify the boot log
# line "🛡️  /v1 engines: ... auth=REQUIRED (verified session)" before assuming
# the gate is working.
INVESTSCAPE_FF_REQUIRE_ENGINE_AUTH=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lighthouse cross-product feature flags
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALL DEFAULT TO DISABLED. Only the exact string "true" enables a flag; "1",
# "yes" and "on" are treated as disabled on purpose.
#
# A flag NEVER bypasses authorization. Enabling one only makes an already fully
# authorized flow reachable — see src/lighthouse/domain/policy.ts.
#
# Do NOT enable any of these without the corresponding Relationship OS contract
# being agreed, the endpoint deployed, and a review sign-off.

# Stage 1 — secure launch receiver, redemption, result callback.
# Blocked on: a database + secret manager in this service, and confirmation of
# the launch-context response shape from Relationship OS.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_STAGE1_LAUNCH_RECEIVER=false

# Stage 2 — cross-product identity linking. Blocked on Relationship OS
# implementing matching invitation/confirmation endpoints.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_CROSS_PRODUCT_IDENTITY_LINKING=false

# Stage 3 — coarse client workspace availability disclosure (consent-gated).
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_CLIENT_WORKSPACE_DISCLOSURE=false

# Stage 4 — client-selected analysis sharing.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_SELECTED_ANALYSIS_SHARING=false

# Stage 5 — professional connectivity projection.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_PROFESSIONAL_CONNECTIVITY_PROJECTION=false

# Stage 6 — cross-product lifecycle/outbox synchronization.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_LIFECYCLE_SYNCHRONIZATION=false

# Stage 7 — cross-product administration surfaces.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_CROSS_PRODUCT_ADMINISTRATION=false

# Stage 8 — delegated client portfolio management (Mode D).
# Blocked on 8 unresolved product decisions in
# DELEGATED_CLIENT_INVESTSCAPE_MANAGEMENT_SCOPE_V0.1.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_DELEGATED_PORTFOLIO_MANAGEMENT=false

# Stage 8 extension — assisted consent.
# Blocked on legal/compliance review. Do not enable.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_ASSISTED_CONSENT=false

# Lighthouse suite entitlement synchronization.
# Blocked on 11 unresolved commercial decisions in
# UNIFIED_LIGHTHOUSE_SUITE_SUBSCRIPTION_SCOPE_V0.1.
LIGHTHOUSE_FF_SUITE_ENTITLEMENT_SYNC=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relationship OS service-to-service credentials — DIRECTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real credentials were issued 2026-09-02 into the gitignored .env. The values
# below are STRUCTURE ONLY — never put real key material in this file.
#
# Per the 2026-09-02 architecture review (P1): "Do not issue one bidirectional
# HMAC secret. Use independent environment-specific credentials for
# Relationship OS -> InvestScape and InvestScape -> Relationship OS."
#
# Two independent 32-byte secrets exist. Each service holds both, in mirrored
# roles, so a key compromised in one direction cannot forge the other:
#
#   InvestScape        INBOUND  = verify from Relationship OS  (key ros-to-is-*)
#                      OUTBOUND = sign to Relationship OS      (key is-to-ros-*)
#   Relationship OS    INBOUND  = verify from InvestScape      (key is-to-ros-*)
#                      OUTBOUND = sign to InvestScape          (key ros-to-is-*)
#
# HMAC canonicalization (pinned to the Relationship OS implementation, and
# reproduced byte-for-byte by our golden-vector test):
#   base      = METHOD \n path \n timestamp \n nonce \n sha256(rawBody).hex
#   signature = HMAC-SHA256(secret, base) -> lowercase hex
#   headers   = x-lighthouse-{service,key-id,timestamp,nonce,signature}
#   skew      = +/- 300s, nonce >= 32 hex chars, replay-protected
#
# ROTATION: the verifier selects a secret BY KEY ID, so current and next keys
# can coexist. Add _NEXT_ variants, deploy to both services, cut the outbound
# key id over, then retire the old pair.

LIGHTHOUSE_SVC_INBOUND_KEY_ID=ros-to-is-YYYYMM-a
LIGHTHOUSE_SVC_INBOUND_SECRET=<64-hex-chars — see .env, never commit>

LIGHTHOUSE_SVC_OUTBOUND_KEY_ID=is-to-ros-YYYYMM-a
LIGHTHOUSE_SVC_OUTBOUND_SECRET=<64-hex-chars — see .env, never commit>

# Optional overlapping keys during rotation.
# LIGHTHOUSE_SVC_INBOUND_NEXT_KEY_ID=
# LIGHTHOUSE_SVC_INBOUND_NEXT_SECRET=

# Base origin of the Relationship OS API. Must be https:// outside localhost.
# Left unset until Relationship OS is deployed — the redemption client returns
# 'unconfigured' and fails closed rather than calling a guessed URL.
# RELATIONSHIP_OS_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (Supabase / Postgres)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required before the Stage 1 receiver can hold a launch-session binding, a
# replay nonce, or an audit row. Absent this, the lighthouse repositories fall
# back to in-memory implementations that are UNSAFE multi-process and are for
# tests and local development only.
#
# Use the session-pooler / direct connection string, not the browser anon key.
# The anon key must never appear in this service.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/postgres

# Set to "true" ONLY in local development to allow the in-memory repository
# fallback. In any deployed environment this must be unset so a missing
# DATABASE_URL fails closed instead of silently running without persistence.
LIGHTHOUSE_ALLOW_INMEMORY_PERSISTENCE=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session verification (Supabase JWT)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/lighthouse/auth/session.ts. Previously undocumented here; written
# down now because INVESTSCAPE_FF_REQUIRE_ENGINE_AUTH above cannot function
# without them — with the flag on and nothing below set, every /v1 request is
# refused with a 503.
#
# Invariant 1: the browser NEVER establishes identity. It presents a bearer
# token; the server verifies the signature and derives the actor reference from
# verified claims. A user id in a body or header is ignored. Supabase `sub` is
# `auth.uid()`, which is exactly the actor_ref lighthouse.context_authorities
# keys on — there is no second identity system.
#
# Nothing is configured by default, and that means UnconfiguredSessionVerifier,
# which rejects every token. Absence of configuration is never permission.

# Expected `iss`. Required for any real verification.
# SUPABASE_JWT_ISSUER=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1

# Expected `aud`. Defaults to "authenticated" when unset.
# SUPABASE_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated

# PREFER THIS: asymmetric verification (RS256/ES256). A leaked public key lets
# an attacker do nothing.
# SUPABASE_JWKS_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json

# Legacy symmetric alternative (HS256). Mutually exclusive with the JWKS URL,
# and strictly worse: a leaked secret lets an attacker MINT sessions. Real
# values belong in the gitignored .env, never here.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=<never commit>

# Local development ONLY: accepts tokens of the form `dev:<actorRef>` with no
# signature at all. Refused outright when NODE_ENV=production. Never set this
# anywhere a real user can reach — it is an identity bypass by design, useful
# solely for exercising the flows end to end without standing up Supabase Auth.
LIGHTHOUSE_ALLOW_DEV_SESSIONS=false
